window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2019-12-3", "source": "2019年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on the importance of having a sense of community responsibility. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "A sense of community responsibility—the awareness that each of us owes something to the neighborhood we\nlive in—lies at the heart of a healthy society. Far from being an empty slogan, it shapes how safe, warm, and\nlivable our shared spaces truly are.\nFor one thing, community responsibility turns strangers into neighbors. When residents voluntarily sort their\nrubbish, watch over one another's children, or join weekend clean-ups, small acts accumulate into a strong\nsense of belonging and collective wellbeing. Take my own compound: after a few families organized a\nvolunteer team to repair the broken playground, others were inspired to follow, and the whole area soon felt\ncleaner and friendlier. Moreover, such shared effort eases pressure on public services, since problems are\nsolved by residents themselves rather than left to overstretched authorities.\nIn short, community responsibility is not a burden but the glue that holds people together. If each of us\ncontributes a little civic awareness, our communities will reward us with a place worth calling home."}, "translation": {"prompt": "荷花(lotus flower)是中国的名花之一，深受人们喜爱。中国许多地方的湖泊和池塘都适宜荷花生长。荷花色彩鲜艳，夏日清晨绽放，夜晚闭合。花期长达两三个月，吸引来自各地的游客前往观赏。荷花具有多种功能，既能绿化水面，又能美化庭园，还可净化水质、减少污染、改善环境。荷花迎骄阳而不惧，出污泥而不染，象征纯洁、高雅，常常用来比喻人的高尚品德，历来是诗人画家创作的重要题材。荷花盛开的地方也是许多摄影爱好者经常光顾之地。", "sample": "The lotus flower, one of China's most famous flowers, is deeply loved by people. Lakes and ponds in many parts of China are suitable for the growth of the lotus. It has bright colours, opening in the early summer mornings and closing at night. Its flowering period lasts as long as two or three months, attracting tourists from all over the country to come and enjoy it. The lotus serves many functions: it can add greenery to the water surface, beautify gardens, purify water, reduce pollution and improve the environment. Standing unafraid under the scorching sun and remaining unstained though it grows out of the mud, the lotus symbolizes purity and elegance; it is often used to describe people's noble character and has long been an important theme for poets and painters. Places where lotuses are in full bloom are also favourite spots frequently visited by many photography enthusiasts."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "The number of devices you can talk to is multiplying—first it was your phone, then your car, and now you can tell your kitchen appliance what to do. But even without gadgets that understand our spoken commands, research suggests that, as bizarre as it sounds, under certain  __26__  , people regularly ascribe human traits to everyday objects.\nSometimes we see things as human because we are  __27__  . In one experiment, people who reported feeling isolated were more likely than others to attribute  __28__  to various gadgets. In turn, feeling close to objects can  __29__  loneliness. When college students were reminded of a time they had been  __30__  in a social setting they compensated by exaggerating their number of friends—unless they were first given tasks that caused them to interact with their phone as if it had human qualities. The phone  __31__  stood in for real friends.\nAt other times, we personify products in an effort to understand them. One study found that three in four respondents yelled at their computer. Further, the more their computer gave them problems, the more likely the respondents were to report that it had its own \"beliefs and  __32__  \".\nSo how do people assign traits to an object? In part, we rely on looks. On humans, wide faces are  __33__  with dominance. Similarly, people rated cars, clocks and watches with wide faces as more dominant-looking than narrow-faced ones, and preferred them—especially in  __34__  situations. An analysis of car sales in Germany found that cars with grilles(护栅) that were upturned like smiles sold best. The purchasers saw this  __35__  as increasing a car's friendliness.", "matchIntro": "Why More Farmers Are Making The Switch to Grass-Fed Meat and Dairy", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Though he didn't come from a farming family, from a young age Tim Joseph was fascinated by the idea of living off the land. Reading magazines like The Stockman Grass Farmer and Graze, he got hooked on the idea of grass-fed agriculture. The idea that all energy and wealth comes from the sun really intrigued him. He thought the shorter the distance between the sun and the end product, the higher the profit to the farmer."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Joseph wanted to put this theory to the test. In 2009, he and his wife Laura launched Maple Hill Creamery, an organic, all grass-fed yogurt company in northern New York. He quickly learned what the market has demonstrated: Demand for grass-fed products currently exceeds supply. Grass-fed beef is enjoying a 25-30% annual growth rate. Sales of grass-fed yogurt and kefir, on the other hand, have in the last year increased by over 38%. This is in comparison with a drop of just under 1% in the total yogurt and kefir market, according to natural and organic market research company SPINS. Joseph's top priority became getting his hands on enough grass-fed milk to keep customers satisfied, since his own 64-cow herd wasn't going to suffice."}, {"label": "C", "text": "His first partnership was with Paul and Phyllis Amburgh, owners of the Dharma Lea farm in New York. The Amburghs, too, were true believers in grass-fed. In addition to supplying milk from their own 85-head herd, they began to help other farmers in the area convert from conventional to certified organic and grass-fed in order to enter the Maple Hill supply chain. Since 2010, the couple has helped 125 small dairy farms convert to grass-fed, with more than 80% of those farms coming on board during the last two years."}, {"label": "D", "text": "All this conversion has helped Maple Hill grow 40-50% every year since it began, with no end in sight. Joseph has learned that a farmer has to have a certain mindset to successfully convert. But convincing open-minded dairy people is actually not that hard, when you look at the economics. Grass-fed milk can fetch up 2.5 times the price of conventional milk. Another factor is the squeeze that conventional dairy farmers have felt as the price of grain they feed their cows has gone up, tightening their profit margins. By replacing expensive grain feed with regenerative management practices, grass-fed farmers are insulated from jumps in the price of feed. These practices include grazing animals on grasses grown from the pastureland's natural seed bank, and fertilized by the cows' own fertilizer."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Champions of this type of regenerative grazing also point to its animal welfare, climate and health benefits: Grass-fed animals live longer out of confinement. Grazing herds stimulate microbial activity in the soil, helping to capture water and separate carbon. And grass-fed dairy and meat have been shown to be higher in certain nutrients and healthy fats."}, {"label": "F", "text": "In the grass fed system, farmers are also not subject to the wildly fluctuating milk prices of the international commodity market. The unpredictability of global demand and the lag-time it takes to add more cows to a herd to meet demand can result in events like the recent cheese surplus. Going grass-fed is a safe refuge, a way for family-scale farms to stay viable. Usually a farmer will get to the point where financially, what they're doing is not working. That's when they call Maple Hill. If the farm is well managed and has enough land, and the desire to convert is sincere, a relationship can begin. Through regular regional educational meetings, a large annual meeting, individual farm visits and thousands of phone calls, the Amburghs pass on the principles of pasture management. Maple Hill signs a contract pledging to buy the farmer's milk at a guaranteed base price, plus quality premiums and incentives for higher protein, butter-fat and other solids."}, {"label": "G", "text": "While Maple Hill's conversion program is unusually hands-on and comprehensive, it's just one of a growing number of businesses committed to slowly changing the way America farms. Joseph calls sharing his knowledge network through peer-to-peer learning a core piece of the company's culture. Last summer, Massachusetts grass-fed beef advocate John Smith launched Big Picture Beef, a network of small grass-fed beef farms in New England and New York that is projected to bring to market 2,500 head of cattle from 125 producers this year. Early indications are that Smith will have no shortage of farm members. Since he began to informally announce the network at farming conferences and on social media, he's received a steady stream of inquiries from interested farmers."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Shinn says he'll provide services ranging from formal seminars to on-farm workshops on holistic management, to one-on-one hand-holding and an almost 24/7 phone hotline for farmers who are converting. In exchange, he guarantees an above-market price for each animal and a calf-to-customer electronic ear tag ID system like that used in the European Union."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Though advocates portray grass fed products as a win-win situation for all, they do have downsides. Price, for one, is an issue. Joseph says his products are priced 10-20% above organic versions, but depending on the product chosen, compared to non-organic conventional yogurt, consumers could pay a premium of 30-50% or more for grass-fed. As for the meat, Smith says his grass-fed hamburger will be priced 20-25% over the conventional alternative. But a look at the prices on online grocer Fresh Direct suggests a grass-fed premium of anywhere from 35-60%."}, {"label": "J", "text": "And not every farmer has the option of going grass-fed. For both beef and dairy production it requires, at least in the beginning, more pastureland. Grass-fed beef production tends to be more labor-intensive as well. But Smith counters that if you factor in the hidden cost of government corn subsidies, environment degradation, and decreased human health and animal welfare, grass-fed is the more cost-effective model. \"The sun provides the lowest cost of production and the cheapest meat,\" he says."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Another grass-fed booster spurring farmers to convert is EPIC, which makes meat-based protein bars. Founders Taylor Collins and his wife, Katie Forrest, used to be endurance athletes; now they're advocates of grass-fed meat. Soon after launching EPIC's most successful product—the Bison Bacon Cranberry Bar—Collins and Forrest found they'd exhausted their sources for bison raised exclusively on pasture. When they started researching the supply chain, they learned that only 2-3% of all bison is actually grass-fed. The rest is feed-lot confined and fed grain and corn."}, {"label": "L", "text": "But after General Mills bought EPIC in 2016, Collins and Forrest suddenly had the resources they needed to expand their supply chain. So the company teamed up with Wisconsin-based rancher Northstar Bison. EPIC fronted the money for the purchase of $2.5 million worth of young bison that will be raised according to its grass-fed protocols, with a guaranteed purchase price. The message to young people who might not otherwise be able to afford to break into the business is, \"'You can purchase this $3 million piece of land here, because I'm guaranteeing you today you'll have 1,000 bison on it.' We're bringing new blood into the old, conventional farming ecosystem, which is really cool to see,\" Collins explains."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Schools are not just a microcosm of society; they mediate it too. The best seek to alleviate the external pressures on their pupils while equipping them better to understand and handle the world outside—at once sheltering them and broadening their horizons. This is ambitious in any circumstances, and in a divided and unequal society the two ideals can clash outright.\nTrips that many adults would consider the adventure of a lifetime—treks in Borneo, a sports tour to Barbados—appear to have become almost routine at some state schools. Parents are being asked for thousands of pounds. Though schools cannot profit from these trips, the companies that arrange them do. Meanwhile, pupils arrive at school hungry because their families can't afford breakfast. The Child Poverty Action Group says nine out of 30 in every classroom fall below the poverty line. The discrepancy is startlingly apparent. Introducing a fundraising requirement for students does not help, as better-off children can tap up richer aunts and neighbours.\nProbing the rock pools of a local beach or practising French on a language exchange can fire children's passions, boost their skills and open their eyes to life's possibilities. Educational outings help bright but disadvantaged students to get better scores in A-level tests. In this globalised age, there is a good case for international travel, and some parents say they can manage the cost of a school trip abroad more easily than a family holiday. Even in the face of immense and mounting financial pressures, some schools have shown remarkable determination and ingenuity in ensuring that all their pupils are able to take up opportunities that may be truly life-changing. They should be applauded. Methods such as whole-school fundraising, with the proceeds pooled, can help to extend opportunities and fuel community spirit.\nBut £3,000 trips cannot be justified when the average income for families with children is just over £30,000. Such initiatives close doors for many pupils. Some parents pull their children out of school because of expensive field trips. Even parents who can see that a trip is little more than a party or celebration may well feel guilt that their child is left behind.\nThe Department for Education's guidance says schools can charge only for board and lodging if the trip is part of the syllabus, and that students receiving government aid are exempt from these costs. However, many schools seem to ignore the advice; and it does not cover the kind of glamorous, exotic trips, which are becoming increasingly common. Schools cannot be expected to bring together communities single-handed. But the least we should expect is that they do not foster divisions and exclude those who are already disadvantaged.", "47": "Schools are not just a microcosm of society; they mediate it too. The best seek to alleviate the external pressures on their pupils while equipping them better to understand and handle the world outside—at once sheltering them and broadening their horizons. This is ambitious in any circumstances, and in a divided and unequal society the two ideals can clash outright.\nTrips that many adults would consider the adventure of a lifetime—treks in Borneo, a sports tour to Barbados—appear to have become almost routine at some state schools. Parents are being asked for thousands of pounds. Though schools cannot profit from these trips, the companies that arrange them do. Meanwhile, pupils arrive at school hungry because their families can't afford breakfast. The Child Poverty Action Group says nine out of 30 in every classroom fall below the poverty line. The discrepancy is startlingly apparent. Introducing a fundraising requirement for students does not help, as better-off children can tap up richer aunts and neighbours.\nProbing the rock pools of a local beach or practising French on a language exchange can fire children's passions, boost their skills and open their eyes to life's possibilities. Educational outings help bright but disadvantaged students to get better scores in A-level tests. In this globalised age, there is a good case for international travel, and some parents say they can manage the cost of a school trip abroad more easily than a family holiday. Even in the face of immense and mounting financial pressures, some schools have shown remarkable determination and ingenuity in ensuring that all their pupils are able to take up opportunities that may be truly life-changing. They should be applauded. Methods such as whole-school fundraising, with the proceeds pooled, can help to extend opportunities and fuel community spirit.\nBut £3,000 trips cannot be justified when the average income for families with children is just over £30,000. Such initiatives close doors for many pupils. Some parents pull their children out of school because of expensive field trips. Even parents who can see that a trip is little more than a party or celebration may well feel guilt that their child is left behind.\nThe Department for Education's guidance says schools can charge only for board and lodging if the trip is part of the syllabus, and that students receiving government aid are exempt from these costs. However, many schools seem to ignore the advice; and it does not cover the kind of glamorous, exotic trips, which are becoming increasingly common. Schools cannot be expected to bring together communities single-handed. But the least we should expect is that they do not foster divisions and exclude those who are already disadvantaged.", "48": "Schools are not just a microcosm of society; they mediate it too. The best seek to alleviate the external pressures on their pupils while equipping them better to understand and handle the world outside—at once sheltering them and broadening their horizons. This is ambitious in any circumstances, and in a divided and unequal society the two ideals can clash outright.\nTrips that many adults would consider the adventure of a lifetime—treks in Borneo, a sports tour to Barbados—appear to have become almost routine at some state schools. Parents are being asked for thousands of pounds. Though schools cannot profit from these trips, the companies that arrange them do. Meanwhile, pupils arrive at school hungry because their families can't afford breakfast. The Child Poverty Action Group says nine out of 30 in every classroom fall below the poverty line. The discrepancy is startlingly apparent. Introducing a fundraising requirement for students does not help, as better-off children can tap up richer aunts and neighbours.\nProbing the rock pools of a local beach or practising French on a language exchange can fire children's passions, boost their skills and open their eyes to life's possibilities. Educational outings help bright but disadvantaged students to get better scores in A-level tests. In this globalised age, there is a good case for international travel, and some parents say they can manage the cost of a school trip abroad more easily than a family holiday. Even in the face of immense and mounting financial pressures, some schools have shown remarkable determination and ingenuity in ensuring that all their pupils are able to take up opportunities that may be truly life-changing. They should be applauded. Methods such as whole-school fundraising, with the proceeds pooled, can help to extend opportunities and fuel community spirit.\nBut £3,000 trips cannot be justified when the average income for families with children is just over £30,000. Such initiatives close doors for many pupils. Some parents pull their children out of school because of expensive field trips. Even parents who can see that a trip is little more than a party or celebration may well feel guilt that their child is left behind.\nThe Department for Education's guidance says schools can charge only for board and lodging if the trip is part of the syllabus, and that students receiving government aid are exempt from these costs. However, many schools seem to ignore the advice; and it does not cover the kind of glamorous, exotic trips, which are becoming increasingly common. Schools cannot be expected to bring together communities single-handed. But the least we should expect is that they do not foster divisions and exclude those who are already disadvantaged.", "49": "Schools are not just a microcosm of society; they mediate it too. The best seek to alleviate the external pressures on their pupils while equipping them better to understand and handle the world outside—at once sheltering them and broadening their horizons. This is ambitious in any circumstances, and in a divided and unequal society the two ideals can clash outright.\nTrips that many adults would consider the adventure of a lifetime—treks in Borneo, a sports tour to Barbados—appear to have become almost routine at some state schools. Parents are being asked for thousands of pounds. Though schools cannot profit from these trips, the companies that arrange them do. Meanwhile, pupils arrive at school hungry because their families can't afford breakfast. The Child Poverty Action Group says nine out of 30 in every classroom fall below the poverty line. The discrepancy is startlingly apparent. Introducing a fundraising requirement for students does not help, as better-off children can tap up richer aunts and neighbours.\nProbing the rock pools of a local beach or practising French on a language exchange can fire children's passions, boost their skills and open their eyes to life's possibilities. Educational outings help bright but disadvantaged students to get better scores in A-level tests. In this globalised age, there is a good case for international travel, and some parents say they can manage the cost of a school trip abroad more easily than a family holiday. Even in the face of immense and mounting financial pressures, some schools have shown remarkable determination and ingenuity in ensuring that all their pupils are able to take up opportunities that may be truly life-changing. They should be applauded. Methods such as whole-school fundraising, with the proceeds pooled, can help to extend opportunities and fuel community spirit.\nBut £3,000 trips cannot be justified when the average income for families with children is just over £30,000. Such initiatives close doors for many pupils. Some parents pull their children out of school because of expensive field trips. Even parents who can see that a trip is little more than a party or celebration may well feel guilt that their child is left behind.\nThe Department for Education's guidance says schools can charge only for board and lodging if the trip is part of the syllabus, and that students receiving government aid are exempt from these costs. However, many schools seem to ignore the advice; and it does not cover the kind of glamorous, exotic trips, which are becoming increasingly common. Schools cannot be expected to bring together communities single-handed. But the least we should expect is that they do not foster divisions and exclude those who are already disadvantaged.", "50": "Schools are not just a microcosm of society; they mediate it too. The best seek to alleviate the external pressures on their pupils while equipping them better to understand and handle the world outside—at once sheltering them and broadening their horizons. This is ambitious in any circumstances, and in a divided and unequal society the two ideals can clash outright.\nTrips that many adults would consider the adventure of a lifetime—treks in Borneo, a sports tour to Barbados—appear to have become almost routine at some state schools. Parents are being asked for thousands of pounds. Though schools cannot profit from these trips, the companies that arrange them do. Meanwhile, pupils arrive at school hungry because their families can't afford breakfast. The Child Poverty Action Group says nine out of 30 in every classroom fall below the poverty line. The discrepancy is startlingly apparent. Introducing a fundraising requirement for students does not help, as better-off children can tap up richer aunts and neighbours.\nProbing the rock pools of a local beach or practising French on a language exchange can fire children's passions, boost their skills and open their eyes to life's possibilities. Educational outings help bright but disadvantaged students to get better scores in A-level tests. In this globalised age, there is a good case for international travel, and some parents say they can manage the cost of a school trip abroad more easily than a family holiday. Even in the face of immense and mounting financial pressures, some schools have shown remarkable determination and ingenuity in ensuring that all their pupils are able to take up opportunities that may be truly life-changing. They should be applauded. Methods such as whole-school fundraising, with the proceeds pooled, can help to extend opportunities and fuel community spirit.\nBut £3,000 trips cannot be justified when the average income for families with children is just over £30,000. Such initiatives close doors for many pupils. Some parents pull their children out of school because of expensive field trips. Even parents who can see that a trip is little more than a party or celebration may well feel guilt that their child is left behind.\nThe Department for Education's guidance says schools can charge only for board and lodging if the trip is part of the syllabus, and that students receiving government aid are exempt from these costs. However, many schools seem to ignore the advice; and it does not cover the kind of glamorous, exotic trips, which are becoming increasingly common. Schools cannot be expected to bring together communities single-handed. But the least we should expect is that they do not foster divisions and exclude those who are already disadvantaged.", "51": "Rising temperatures and overfishing in the pristine(未受污染的) waters around the Antarctic could see king penguin populations pushed to the brink of extinction by the end of the century, according to a new study. The study's report states that as global warming transforms the environment in the world's last great wilderness, 70 percent of king penguins could either disappear or be forced to find new breeding grounds.\nCo-author Celine Le Bohec, from the University of Strasbourg in France, warned: \"If there are no actions aimed at halting or controlling global warming, and the pace of the current human-induced changes such as climate change and overfishing stays the same, the species may soon disappear.\" The findings come amid growing concern over the future of the Antarctic. Earlier this month a separate study found that a combination of climate change and industrial fishing is threatening the krill(磷虾) population in Antarctic waters, with a potentially disastrous impact on whales, seals and penguins. But today's report is the starkest warning yet of the potentially devastating impact of climate change and human exploitation on the Antarctic's delicate ecosystems.\nLe Bohec said: \"Unless current greenhouse gas emissions drop, 70 percent of king penguins—1.1 million breeding pairs—will be forced to relocate their breeding grounds, or face extinction by 2100.\" King penguins are the second-largest type of penguin and only breed on specific isolated islands in the Southern Ocean where there is no ice cover and easy access to the sea. As the ocean warms, a body of water called the Antarctic Polar Front—an upward movement of nutrient-rich sea that supports a huge abundance of marine life—is being pushed further south. This means that king penguins, which feed on fish and krill in this body of water, have to travel further to their feeding grounds, leaving their hungry chicks for longer. And as the distance between their breeding grounds and their food grows, entire colonies could be wiped out.\nLe Bohec said: \"The plight of the king penguin should serve as a warning about the future of the entire marine environment in the Antarctic. Penguins, like other seabirds and marine mammals, occupy higher levels in the food chain and they are what we call bio-indicators of their ecosystems.\" Penguins are sensitive indicators of changes in marine ecosystems. As such, they are key species for understanding and predicting impacts of global change on Antarctic and sub-Antarctic marine ecosystems. The report found that although some king penguins may be able to relocate to new breeding grounds closer to their retreating food source, suitable new habitats would be scarce. Only a handful of islands in the Southern Ocean are suitable for sustaining large breeding colonies.", "52": "Rising temperatures and overfishing in the pristine(未受污染的) waters around the Antarctic could see king penguin populations pushed to the brink of extinction by the end of the century, according to a new study. The study's report states that as global warming transforms the environment in the world's last great wilderness, 70 percent of king penguins could either disappear or be forced to find new breeding grounds.\nCo-author Celine Le Bohec, from the University of Strasbourg in France, warned: \"If there are no actions aimed at halting or controlling global warming, and the pace of the current human-induced changes such as climate change and overfishing stays the same, the species may soon disappear.\" The findings come amid growing concern over the future of the Antarctic. Earlier this month a separate study found that a combination of climate change and industrial fishing is threatening the krill(磷虾) population in Antarctic waters, with a potentially disastrous impact on whales, seals and penguins. But today's report is the starkest warning yet of the potentially devastating impact of climate change and human exploitation on the Antarctic's delicate ecosystems.\nLe Bohec said: \"Unless current greenhouse gas emissions drop, 70 percent of king penguins—1.1 million breeding pairs—will be forced to relocate their breeding grounds, or face extinction by 2100.\" King penguins are the second-largest type of penguin and only breed on specific isolated islands in the Southern Ocean where there is no ice cover and easy access to the sea. As the ocean warms, a body of water called the Antarctic Polar Front—an upward movement of nutrient-rich sea that supports a huge abundance of marine life—is being pushed further south. This means that king penguins, which feed on fish and krill in this body of water, have to travel further to their feeding grounds, leaving their hungry chicks for longer. And as the distance between their breeding grounds and their food grows, entire colonies could be wiped out.\nLe Bohec said: \"The plight of the king penguin should serve as a warning about the future of the entire marine environment in the Antarctic. Penguins, like other seabirds and marine mammals, occupy higher levels in the food chain and they are what we call bio-indicators of their ecosystems.\" Penguins are sensitive indicators of changes in marine ecosystems. As such, they are key species for understanding and predicting impacts of global change on Antarctic and sub-Antarctic marine ecosystems. The report found that although some king penguins may be able to relocate to new breeding grounds closer to their retreating food source, suitable new habitats would be scarce. Only a handful of islands in the Southern Ocean are suitable for sustaining large breeding colonies.", "53": "Rising temperatures and overfishing in the pristine(未受污染的) waters around the Antarctic could see king penguin populations pushed to the brink of extinction by the end of the century, according to a new study. The study's report states that as global warming transforms the environment in the world's last great wilderness, 70 percent of king penguins could either disappear or be forced to find new breeding grounds.\nCo-author Celine Le Bohec, from the University of Strasbourg in France, warned: \"If there are no actions aimed at halting or controlling global warming, and the pace of the current human-induced changes such as climate change and overfishing stays the same, the species may soon disappear.\" The findings come amid growing concern over the future of the Antarctic. Earlier this month a separate study found that a combination of climate change and industrial fishing is threatening the krill(磷虾) population in Antarctic waters, with a potentially disastrous impact on whales, seals and penguins. But today's report is the starkest warning yet of the potentially devastating impact of climate change and human exploitation on the Antarctic's delicate ecosystems.\nLe Bohec said: \"Unless current greenhouse gas emissions drop, 70 percent of king penguins—1.1 million breeding pairs—will be forced to relocate their breeding grounds, or face extinction by 2100.\" King penguins are the second-largest type of penguin and only breed on specific isolated islands in the Southern Ocean where there is no ice cover and easy access to the sea. As the ocean warms, a body of water called the Antarctic Polar Front—an upward movement of nutrient-rich sea that supports a huge abundance of marine life—is being pushed further south. This means that king penguins, which feed on fish and krill in this body of water, have to travel further to their feeding grounds, leaving their hungry chicks for longer. And as the distance between their breeding grounds and their food grows, entire colonies could be wiped out.\nLe Bohec said: \"The plight of the king penguin should serve as a warning about the future of the entire marine environment in the Antarctic. Penguins, like other seabirds and marine mammals, occupy higher levels in the food chain and they are what we call bio-indicators of their ecosystems.\" Penguins are sensitive indicators of changes in marine ecosystems. As such, they are key species for understanding and predicting impacts of global change on Antarctic and sub-Antarctic marine ecosystems. The report found that although some king penguins may be able to relocate to new breeding grounds closer to their retreating food source, suitable new habitats would be scarce. Only a handful of islands in the Southern Ocean are suitable for sustaining large breeding colonies.", "54": "Rising temperatures and overfishing in the pristine(未受污染的) waters around the Antarctic could see king penguin populations pushed to the brink of extinction by the end of the century, according to a new study. The study's report states that as global warming transforms the environment in the world's last great wilderness, 70 percent of king penguins could either disappear or be forced to find new breeding grounds.\nCo-author Celine Le Bohec, from the University of Strasbourg in France, warned: \"If there are no actions aimed at halting or controlling global warming, and the pace of the current human-induced changes such as climate change and overfishing stays the same, the species may soon disappear.\" The findings come amid growing concern over the future of the Antarctic. Earlier this month a separate study found that a combination of climate change and industrial fishing is threatening the krill(磷虾) population in Antarctic waters, with a potentially disastrous impact on whales, seals and penguins. But today's report is the starkest warning yet of the potentially devastating impact of climate change and human exploitation on the Antarctic's delicate ecosystems.\nLe Bohec said: \"Unless current greenhouse gas emissions drop, 70 percent of king penguins—1.1 million breeding pairs—will be forced to relocate their breeding grounds, or face extinction by 2100.\" King penguins are the second-largest type of penguin and only breed on specific isolated islands in the Southern Ocean where there is no ice cover and easy access to the sea. As the ocean warms, a body of water called the Antarctic Polar Front—an upward movement of nutrient-rich sea that supports a huge abundance of marine life—is being pushed further south. This means that king penguins, which feed on fish and krill in this body of water, have to travel further to their feeding grounds, leaving their hungry chicks for longer. And as the distance between their breeding grounds and their food grows, entire colonies could be wiped out.\nLe Bohec said: \"The plight of the king penguin should serve as a warning about the future of the entire marine environment in the Antarctic. Penguins, like other seabirds and marine mammals, occupy higher levels in the food chain and they are what we call bio-indicators of their ecosystems.\" Penguins are sensitive indicators of changes in marine ecosystems. As such, they are key species for understanding and predicting impacts of global change on Antarctic and sub-Antarctic marine ecosystems. The report found that although some king penguins may be able to relocate to new breeding grounds closer to their retreating food source, suitable new habitats would be scarce. Only a handful of islands in the Southern Ocean are suitable for sustaining large breeding colonies.", "55": "Rising temperatures and overfishing in the pristine(未受污染的) waters around the Antarctic could see king penguin populations pushed to the brink of extinction by the end of the century, according to a new study. The study's report states that as global warming transforms the environment in the world's last great wilderness, 70 percent of king penguins could either disappear or be forced to find new breeding grounds.\nCo-author Celine Le Bohec, from the University of Strasbourg in France, warned: \"If there are no actions aimed at halting or controlling global warming, and the pace of the current human-induced changes such as climate change and overfishing stays the same, the species may soon disappear.\" The findings come amid growing concern over the future of the Antarctic. Earlier this month a separate study found that a combination of climate change and industrial fishing is threatening the krill(磷虾) population in Antarctic waters, with a potentially disastrous impact on whales, seals and penguins. But today's report is the starkest warning yet of the potentially devastating impact of climate change and human exploitation on the Antarctic's delicate ecosystems.\nLe Bohec said: \"Unless current greenhouse gas emissions drop, 70 percent of king penguins—1.1 million breeding pairs—will be forced to relocate their breeding grounds, or face extinction by 2100.\" King penguins are the second-largest type of penguin and only breed on specific isolated islands in the Southern Ocean where there is no ice cover and easy access to the sea. As the ocean warms, a body of water called the Antarctic Polar Front—an upward movement of nutrient-rich sea that supports a huge abundance of marine life—is being pushed further south. This means that king penguins, which feed on fish and krill in this body of water, have to travel further to their feeding grounds, leaving their hungry chicks for longer. And as the distance between their breeding grounds and their food grows, entire colonies could be wiped out.\nLe Bohec said: \"The plight of the king penguin should serve as a warning about the future of the entire marine environment in the Antarctic. Penguins, like other seabirds and marine mammals, occupy higher levels in the food chain and they are what we call bio-indicators of their ecosystems.\" Penguins are sensitive indicators of changes in marine ecosystems. As such, they are key species for understanding and predicting impacts of global change on Antarctic and sub-Antarctic marine ecosystems. The report found that although some king penguins may be able to relocate to new breeding grounds closer to their retreating food source, suitable new habitats would be scarce. Only a handful of islands in the Southern Ocean are suitable for sustaining large breeding colonies."}, "questions": [{"id": "2019-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) circumstances\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 under + 形容词 certain 后必为名词\n【依据】under certain circumstances 是固定搭配\nunder certain circumstances（在某些情况下）是英语里高频固定短语，六级阅读 / 写作\n里几乎是模板词。语义上也最贴：整段在说\"在某些条件下，人们会拟人化日常物品\"。\n【竞争词】H) consciousness 意识不与 under certain 共现\nconsciousness（意识）不能跟 under certain 形成自然短语；而且它在 32 题描述电脑\"有自己\n的信念和欲望 / 意识\"那种语境里更对位（实际 32 题填 desires）。\nI) desires 搭配不成立、且后文有更对位的空\nunder certain desires 不是英语习惯说法。desires 在 32 题与 beliefs and ___ 构成\"信念和欲\n望\"才是它真正的位置。\nK) feature feature 单数不接 under certain\nfeature 表\"特征\"，搭配是 a feature of，不与 under certain 同现；而且单数形式形态上也不\n像。它在 35 题 saw this ___ 才是合身的位置。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 under + 形容词 certa"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) lonely\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词后接表语，要形容词\n【依据】下一句\"isolated 的人更易拟人化\"是同义印证\n紧跟的下一句给出了实验佐证：people who reported feeling isolated were more likely\nthan others to attribute __(28)__ to various gadgets。isolated（被孤立的）正是 lonely\n的近义改写——六级常见的同义替换信号，直接锁定 L。\n【竞争词】C) arrogant 语义与下文实验对不上\narrogant 是\"傲慢\"，与第 2 段的孤独 / 隔绝主题毫无衔接，更不能解释为何要把物当人。\nF) competitive 好胜与拟人化无逻辑链\n好胜与\"把物品看成人\"没有任何因果关系，下文也未提竞争语境；后面第 4 段的 competitive\nsituations 才是它真正归宿。\nM) separate 搭配怪异、词义不对人\nseparate 通常修饰物或空间上的\"分开\"，we are separate 既不像地道英语，也无法解释下句\n的 isolated。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词后接表语，要形容词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) consciousness\n名词\n【词性槽】动词 attribute 后接宾语，要名词\n【依据】把\"意识\"归给小工具正是拟人化\n通篇主旨是 people regularly ascribe human traits to everyday objects——人把人类特\n有的属性赋给物品。最具有\"人类专属\"意味的就是 consciousness（意识）。把\"意识\"归给\ngadgets 正是\"赋人类属性\"的典型表现。\n【竞争词】I) desires desires 已被 32 题预留\ndesires 也能与拟人化挂钩，但 32 题 beliefs and __(32)__ 是它最贴的位置（与 beliefs 并\n列），不能两处都填。本题先做的话也通，但等做到 32 题就会发现 desires 走不掉、只能回头\n改 28 题——这种回炒成本提示我们 28 题应该让位给 H。\nK) feature feature 不属\"人类专属\"属性\nfeature 是中性词\"特征\"，物品本身也有特征，谈不上\"把它归给小工具\"的拟人化意味，与主旨\n脱节。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— desires 与 consciousness 都能填\"拟人化属性\"，容易选\n错。to attribute __(28)__ to various gadgets 这一空，desires（欲望）和 consciousness\n（意识）都是\"人类专属属性\"，词性也都对。考生若按题号顺序硬填，很容易先把 desires\n用在 28 题，结果到 32 题 beliefs and __(32)__ 时发现 desires 已用、被迫错选。防坑方\n法：通读全文后留意\"对应程度更高\"的空——beliefs and ___ 的并列对称硬框架明显比\nattribute ___ to gadgets 的宽泛宾语位更强约束，desires 应优先服务 32 题。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·动词 attribute 后接宾语，要名"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) alleviate\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 can 后接动词原形\n【依据】alleviate loneliness 是高频搭配，语义贴段意\nalleviate 是\"减轻、缓解\"的正式表达，常与痛苦 / 孤独 / 压力等抽象不适搭配。整句\"与物\n品建立亲近感能 ___ 孤独\"，下文又用\"手机替代真实朋友\"的实验佐证孤独被缓解了——所\n以本空填\"减轻\"。\n【竞争词】O) warrant warrant loneliness 逻辑相反\nwarrant 意为\"使……变得有必要 / 值得\"，warrant loneliness 等于\"让孤独有必要\"——和段\n落\"亲近物品能补偿孤独\"的逻辑完全相反，正话反说就是六级常见陷阱。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— warrant 与 alleviate 都能填 can ___ loneliness 的动词\n槽，但方向相反。can __(29)__ loneliness 这一空，词性筛完后留下 warrant 和 alleviate\n两个动词原形，看上去都通。但 warrant loneliness 是\"让孤独变得有必要\"，与段落\"亲近\n物品能补偿孤独\"的方向相反——正话反说是六级最毒的陷阱。防坑方法：动词空选完必须\n再校验一次段意方向，问自己\"这是加重还是减轻？\"", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 can 后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) excluded\n动词\n【词性槽】had been ___ 是被动过去完成的过去分词位\n【依据】上下文是\"被社交排斥\"才需补偿\n整句后文是 they compensated by exaggerating their number of friends——之所以要\n靠夸大朋友数来补偿，前提一定是\"被排斥\"。excluded（被排除、被排斥）正好接上这个心\n理逻辑链；而且与本段开头的 lonely / isolated 一脉相承，都是\"孤独 / 排斥\"主题词。\n【竞争词】D) associated associated 不与社交排斥语境共现\nassociated 后一般接 with，单独说 \"had been associated in a social setting\" 既不\n符合搭配也无法解释为何要\"夸大朋友数补偿\"。它真正归宿在第 4 段的 are associated\nwith dominance。\nG) conceded concede 词义对不上\nconcede 是\"承认 / 让步\"，被动 \"had been conceded\" 意思扭曲，与\"在社交场合被怎样\"完全\n脱节。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·had been ___ 是被动过去完成"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) apparently\n副词\n【词性槽】主语和谓语动词之间作状语，要副词\n【依据】apparently = \"看上去 / 据观察\"，与实验结论的语气吻合\n整段在转述心理学实验：手机被发现替代了真实朋友。这是研究者基于行为现象做的推断式\n结论，用 apparently（显然 / 据看上去）作语气标记最贴。整句\"手机似乎替代了真实朋\n友\"是科普文常见的谨慎陈述。\n【竞争词】N) spectacularly 程度过强、与谨慎语气冲突\nspectacularly\"惊人地、引人注目地\"是程度极强的副词，与心理实验\"谨慎陈述发现\"的语气冲\n突；六级文本里科研结论几乎不用这种夸张副词修饰。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语和谓语动词之间作状语，要副词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) desires\n名词\n【词性槽】and 并列 beliefs，必须同为名词复数\n【依据】beliefs and desires 是哲学 / 心理学高频搭配\nbeliefs and desires（信念与欲望）是描述\"心智状态\"的经典并列。电脑被赋予\"信念和欲\n望\"，正是用拟人化语言去解释\"它为什么老给我使坏\"的心理。\n【竞争词】K) feature 单复数与并列对称都不合\nfeature 是单数名词，与复数 beliefs 并列既不对称，也丢了\"人类心智专属\"的意味。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·and 并列 beliefs，必须同为名"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) associated\n动词\n【词性槽】are ___ with 是被动结构\n【依据】be associated with 是固定搭配\nbe associated with（与……相关联）是六级阅读高频被动短语。语义上\"宽脸与支配性相关\n联\"符合段落\"靠外观推断人格\"的论点，是科普文里描述相关关系的标准说法。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·are ___ with 是被动结构"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) competitive\n形容词\n【词性槽】in ___ situations，形容词修饰名词\n【依据】宽脸代表支配感，在竞争场合更被偏爱\n前句已说 On humans, wide faces are __(33)__ with dominance——宽脸与支配性 / 强势\n挂钩。强势特征自然在\"竞争性\"场合更受偏爱：competitive situations（竞争场合）。这\n是常识 + 段落逻辑的双重锁定。\n【竞争词】C) arrogant arrogant 修饰人不修饰场合\narrogant situations 不是英语习惯说法，arrogant 一般描述人的态度，不修饰\"情境\"。\nM) separate separate situations 语义脱节\nseparate situations\"分开的情境\"既无搭配先例，也与\"宽脸 = 支配性 = 在某种场合更被偏\n爱\"的因果链对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·in ___ situations，形容"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alleviate", "B) apparently", "C) arrogant", "D) associated", "E) circumstances", "F) competitive", "G) conceded", "H) consciousness", "I) desires", "J) excluded", "K) feature", "L) lonely", "M) separate", "N) spectacularly", "O) warrant"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) feature\n名词\n【词性槽】this + 空作宾语，要单数名词\n【依据】feature 正是回指\"上扬护栅\"这一外观特征\n前句 cars with grilles\\(护栅\\) that were upturned like smiles sold best 提到的\"微笑式上\n扬护栅\"是一种外观特征。本句 this ___ 自然指代它，用 feature（特征）回指最贴切，且和\n段落主题\"靠外观赋予物品人格\"相合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·this + 空作宾语，要单数名词"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Farmers going grass-fed are not affected by the ever-changing milk prices of the global market.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】国际市场奶价\n【定位】In the grass fed system, farmers are also not subject to the wildly fluctuating milk prices of\nthe international commodity market.\n在草饲体系下，农户也不受国际大宗商品市场剧烈波动的奶价影响。\n【改写】Farmers going grass-fed ↔ In the grass fed system, farmers\n改写身份描述\nnot affected by ↔ not subject to\n同义短语替换\nthe ever-changing milk prices of the global market ↔\nthe wildly fluctuating milk prices of the international commodity market\n同义形容词 + 同义市场限定\n【辨邻】 D 本段也讲到价格变动，但落点在 the price of grain they feed their cows has gone\nup——草饲农户'免于'的是饲料价格波动，不是国际市场奶价；与陈述句的 milk\nprices of the global market 错位。\nBy replacing expensive grain feed with regenerative management practices, grass-fed farmers are\ninsulated from jumps in the price of feed.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Over the years, Tim Joseph's partners have helped many dairy farmers to switch to grass-fed.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Tim Joseph 的合伙人帮助多家奶农转型\n【定位】Since 2010, the couple has helped 125 small dairy farms convert to grass-fed, with more than\n80% of those farms coming on board during the last two years.\n自 2010 年起，这对夫妇已帮助 125 家小型奶牛场转型为草饲，其中 80% 以上是在过去两\n年内加入的。\n【改写】Tim Joseph's partners ↔ His first partnership was with Paul and Phyllis Amburgh\n代词 + 同根词回指 Joseph\nhelped many dairy farmers to switch to grass-fed ↔\nhelped 125 small dairy farms convert to grass-fed\n概括上位 + 同义动词\nOver the years ↔ Since 2010\n时间表达同义化\n【辨邻】 G 本段虽也讲到帮助小农户进入草饲网络，但主语是 Massachusetts grass-fed beef\nadvocate John Smith 创办的 Big Picture Beef，与陈题的'Tim Joseph's partners'施\n事不一致。\nMassachusetts grass-fed beef advocate John Smith launched Big Picture Beef, a network of small\ngrass-fed beef farms in New England and New York", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "One advocate believes that many other benefits should be taken into consideration when we assess the cost-effectiveness of grass-fed farming.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】倡导者主张算入其他收益\n【定位】But Smith counters that if you factor in the hidden cost of government corn subsidies,\nenvironment degradation, and decreased human health and animal welfare, grass-fed is the\nmore cost-effective model.\n但 Smith 反驳说，如果把政府玉米补贴、环境退化、人类健康与动物福利下降这些隐性成\n本算进去，草饲反而是更具成本效益的模式。\n【改写】One advocate ↔ Smith\n具体人物上位为'一位倡导者'\nother benefits should be taken into consideration ↔\nif you factor in the hidden cost of government corn subsidies, environment degradation, and\ndecreased human health and animal welfare\n'taken into consideration'='factor in'+条件句还原\ncost-effectiveness of grass-fed farming ↔ grass-fed is the more cost-effective model\n同根词同义\n【辨邻】 E E 段列举 animal welfare, climate and health benefits 等好处，但既没有出现'cost-\neffective'这类评估框架词，也没有'某位倡导者主张'这种立场表态，缺少陈题里'other\nbenefits should be taken into consideration when we assess the cost-\neffectiveness'的核心论证结构。\nChampions of this type of regenerative grazing also point to its animal welfare, climate and\nhealth benefits", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Many dairy farmers were persuaded to switch to grass-fed when they saw its advantage in terms of profits.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】利润优势说服奶农转型\n【定位】But convincing open-minded dairy people is actually not that hard, when you look at the\neconomics. Grass-fed milk can fetch up 2.5 times the price of conventional milk.\n但只要看一眼经济账，说服开明的奶农其实并不难。草饲奶能卖到常规奶 2.5 倍的价格。\n【改写】Many dairy farmers were persuaded to switch to grass-fed ↔\nconvincing open-minded dairy people is actually not that hard\n主被动转换 + 同义动词\nits advantage in terms of profits ↔\nGrass-fed milk can fetch up 2.5 times the price of conventional milk\n具体经济账抽象为利润优势\nwhen they saw ↔ when you look at the economics\n同义动词 + 同义条件\n【辨邻】 F F 段说 Usually a farmer will get to the point where financially, what they're\ndoing is not working. That's when they call Maple Hill，这是因为常规模式做不下\n去才转型，与陈题强调的'看到利润优势'的主动吸引不一致。\nGoing grass-fed is a safe refuge, a way for family-scale farms to stay viable\nI I 段说 they do have downsides. Price, for one, is an issue，把价格当作缺点讨论，\n主语是消费者承担的溢价，不是奶农获得的利润优势。\nconsumers could pay a premium of 30-50% or more for grass-fed", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Tim Joseph's grass-fed program is only one example of how American farming practice is changing.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Tim Joseph 的项目只是众多例子之一\n【定位】While Maple Hill's conversion program is unusually hands-on and comprehensive, it's\njust one of a growing number of businesses committed to slowly changing the way\nAmerica farms.\n虽然 Maple Hill 的转型项目格外亲力亲为、覆盖周到，但它只是越来越多致力于慢慢改变\n美国农业方式的企业中的一个。\n【改写】Tim Joseph's grass-fed program ↔ Maple Hill's conversion program\nMaple Hill = Joseph 的公司，回指替换\nonly one example ↔ it's just one of a growing number of businesses\n范围词同义改写\nhow American farming practice is changing ↔ changing the way America farms\n名词化与动词化的句式重构\n【辨邻】 K K 段开头说 Another grass-fed booster spurring farmers to convert is EPIC，虽然\n有'另一家'这个暗示存在多家，但叙述焦点是 EPIC 自己的发现，没有出现'Maple\nHill/Joseph 项目仅是其中之一'这种结构化概括。\nAnother grass-fed booster spurring farmers to convert is EPIC", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Tim Joseph was fascinated by the notion that sunlight brings energy and wealth to mankind.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】阳光带来能量与财富\n【定位】The idea that all energy and wealth comes from the sun really intrigued him.\n所有能量与财富都来自太阳——这个想法真的让他着迷。\n【改写】Tim Joseph was fascinated ↔ really intrigued him\n同义动词 + 主被动转换\nthe notion that sunlight brings energy and wealth to mankind ↔\nThe idea that all energy and wealth comes from the sun\n同义名词 + 来源关系反向", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "One problem with grass-fed products is that they are usually more expensive than conventional ones.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】草饲产品比常规产品贵\n【定位】Though advocates portray grass fed products as a win-win situation for all, they do have\ndownsides. Price, for one, is an issue.\n尽管倡导者把草饲产品描绘成对各方都共赢的局面，但它们确实有缺点。价格就是其中\n之一。\n【改写】One problem with grass-fed products ↔ they do have downsides. Price, for one, is an issue\n同义名词 + 列举词\nthey are usually more expensive than conventional ones ↔\nconsumers could pay a premium of 30-50% or more for grass-fed\n'pay a premium' 即 'more expensive'\n【辨邻】 D D 段虽然写 Grass-fed milk can fetch up 2.5 times the price of conventional\nmilk，但这是作为奶农转型的吸引力来讲的，不是作为'缺点'来讨论。\nGrass-fed milk can fetch up 2.5 times the price of conventional milk.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Grass fed products have proved to be healthier and more nutritious.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】草饲产品更健康更有营养\n【定位】And grass-fed dairy and meat have been shown to be higher in certain nutrients and\nhealthy fats.\n草饲奶制品与肉类已被证实在某些营养与健康脂肪含量上更高。\n【改写】Grass fed products have proved to be ↔ grass-fed dairy and meat have been shown to be\n同义被动短语 + 上位概括\nhealthier and more nutritious ↔ higher in certain nutrients and healthy fats\n同根词形容词与名词互换\n【辨邻】 J J 段只是用 decreased human health and animal welfare 作为反驳论据之一，没有\n像 E 段那样直接对草饲产品做出'更健康、更有营养'的结论性陈述。\nif you factor in the hidden cost of government corn subsidies, environment degradation, and\ndecreased human health and animal welfare", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "When Tim Joseph started his business, he found grass-fed products fell short of demand.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】起步时供不应求\n【定位】He quickly learned what the market has demonstrated: Demand for grass-fed products\ncurrently exceeds supply.\n他很快了解到市场已经表明的事实——草饲产品当前的需求超过了供给。\n【改写】When Tim Joseph started his business ↔\nIn 2009, he and his wife Laura launched Maple Hill Creamery\n上位名词 + 同义动词\ngrass-fed products fell short of demand ↔\nDemand for grass-fed products currently exceeds supply\n供需关系正反表述\nhe found ↔ He quickly learned\n同义动词替换\n【辨邻】 A A 段叙事都在'从年轻时起到读杂志、被理念吸引'这一'创业前'阶段，没有出现'创业后\n发现供不应求'的事实。\nfrom a young age Tim Joseph was fascinated by the idea of living off the land", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "A snack bar producer discovered that the supply of purely grass-fed bison meat was scarce.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】零食棒厂商发现纯草饲野牛肉稀缺\n【定位】Soon after launching EPIC's most successful product—the Bison Bacon Cranberry Bar\n—Collins and Forrest found they'd exhausted their sources for bison raised exclusively\non pasture.\n在推出 EPIC 最成功的产品——野牛培根蔓越莓棒——后不久，Collins 和 Forrest 发现他\n们专门草饲饲养的野牛货源已经用尽。\n【改写】A snack bar producer ↔ EPIC, which makes meat-based protein bars\n上位概括 + 同类名词\ndiscovered that ↔ Collins and Forrest found\n同义动词替换\nthe supply of purely grass-fed bison meat was scarce ↔\nthey'd exhausted their sources for bison raised exclusively on pasture\n'exhausted sources'='supply was scarce'\npurely grass-fed bison meat was scarce ↔ only 2-3% of all bison is actually grass-fed\n比例数据具体化稀缺\n【辨邻】 L L 段说 after General Mills bought EPIC in 2016, Collins and Forrest suddenly\nhad the resources they needed to expand their supply chain，是发现稀缺之后的\n解决方案，与陈题的'发现稀缺'动作不在同一时间节点。\nCollins and Forrest suddenly had the resources they needed to expand their supply chain\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 介绍 Tim Joseph 的成长背景与理念起点：他非农家出身，却因阅读 The Stockman\nGrass Farmer and Graze 等杂志，迷上了草饲农业，并被一种理念深深吸引——所有\n能量与财富都来自太阳，太阳到终产品的距离越短，农户利润越高。\n信息指纹 Tim Joseph 童年起点与太阳能量理念\nB\nTim Joseph 于 2009 年与妻子创办 Maple Hill Creamery，迅速发现草饲产品供不应\n求：草饲牛肉年增长 25-30%，草饲酸奶与开菲尔在上一年增长 38% 以上，而总市场\n反而下降近 1%；自有 64 头牛已不够用。\n信息指纹 创业起点与供不应求数据\nC 首位合作伙伴是 Paul and Phyllis Amburgh 夫妇，他们除自家 85 头牛供奶外，自\n2010 年起帮助 125 家小型奶牛场转型为有机草饲，其中 80% 以上是近两年完成转\n型的。\n信息指纹 Amburgh 夫妇协助 125 家奶农转型\nD\n转型推动 Maple Hill 每年增长 40-50%；说服开明奶农并不难，因为草饲奶可卖到常\n规奶 2.5 倍价格，而饲料粮价上涨挤压了常规奶农利润，草饲农户用再生牧场管理替\n代昂贵谷物饲料，可免于饲料价格波动。\n信息指纹 利润优势促使奶农转型\nE 再生牧场放牧的支持者还指出其动物福利、气候与健康收益：草饲动物寿命更长；放\n牧刺激土壤微生物活动；草饲奶肉在某些营养与健康脂肪含量上更高。\n信息指纹 动物福利气候与营养健康收益\nF\n草饲体系下奶农不受国际大宗商品市场剧烈波动的奶价影响；草饲是家庭农场维持生\n计的安全港；Maple Hill 与转型农户签合同，承诺保底收购价加质量溢价。\n信息指纹 免受国际奶价波动 + 合同保底价\nG\nMaple Hill 的转型项目只是越来越多致力于慢慢改变美国农业方式的企业之一；\nJoseph 把同行互助学习视为公司文化核心；同时介绍 John Smith 在新英格兰创办 Big\nPicture Beef 网络。\n信息指纹 Maple Hill 仅是众多变革企业之一\nH Shinn 承诺为转型农户提供从正式研讨会到一对一手把手、近乎全天候的电话热线等\n服务；作为交换，保证高于市场价的收购价与从小牛到客户的电子耳标追溯系统。\n信息指纹 Shinn 的转型服务与电子耳标\nI 草饲产品也有缺点：价格是一个问题。Joseph 称其产品比有机版贵 10-20%，比非有\n机常规酸奶贵 30-50% 或更多；Smith 的草饲汉堡肉贵 20-25%；Fresh Direct 上的草\n饲溢价高达 35-60%。\n信息指纹 草饲产品价格更贵的具体溢价\nJ 并非每位农户都有走草饲的选择：起步阶段需要更多牧场，草饲牛肉生产更劳动密\n集；但 Smith 反驳称，若把政府玉米补贴、环境退化、人类健康与动物福利的隐性成\n本算进去，草饲反而是更具成本效益的模式。\n信息指纹 把隐性成本算进去后草饲更划算\nK\n另一推动草饲转型的企业 EPIC 由 Taylor Collins 和妻子 Katie Forrest 创办，其爆款\nBison Bacon Cranberry Bar 推出后耗尽了纯草饲野牛货源；他们调研发现全美仅 2-3%\n的野牛是真正草饲饲养。\n信息指纹 蛋白棒厂商 EPIC 发现纯草饲野牛奇缺\nL 2016 年 General Mills 收购 EPIC 后，Collins 夫妇有资源扩展供应链，与 Northstar\nBison 牧场合作，预付 250 万美元采购按草饲规程饲养的小野牛，并保证收购价，帮\n助年轻人进入这个行业。\n信息指纹 EPIC 被收购后预付资金扩张草饲供应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say best schools should do?", "options": ["A) Prepare students to both challenge and change the divided unequal society.", "B) Protect students from social pressures and enable them to face the world.", "C) Motivate students to develop their physical as well as intellectual abilities.", "D) Encourage students to be ambitious and help them to achieve their goals."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say best schools should do? 指向作者对某一具体对象\n（best schools）的明确表述，要找的是原文中可被定位句直接覆盖的客观信息。\n【拆句】主干 What does the author say best schools should do? 是嵌套宾语从句：主句 What\ndoes the author say，宾语从句 best schools should do；理解时把它读作「作者说 best\nschools 该做什么」即可，无陷阱。\n【定位】The best seek to alleviate the external pressures on their pupils while equipping them\nbetter to understand and handle the world outside—at once sheltering them and broadening\ntheir horizons.\n最好的学校力求缓解学生面临的外部压力，同时让他们更好地理解和应对外部世界——既庇\n护他们，又拓宽他们的视野。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n选项把作者所说的「理解和应对外部世界」夸大为 challenge and change the divided\nunequal society，文中只在末段说 Schools cannot be expected to bring together\ncommunities single-handed，明确否定学校独力变革社会。\nB) ✓ 正确\nalleviate the external pressures on their pupils ↔ Protect students from social pressures\nequipping them better to understand and handle the world outside ↔\nenable them to face the world\n预判中「替学生挡压力」对应 Protect students from social pressures，「让他们应对\n外部世界」对应 enable them to face the world，B 项是定位句的完整同义改写。\nC) 无中生有\n原文从未提及 physical as well as intellectual abilities 这一维度，作者讲的是减压 +\n应对世界，不是体能与智力的双轨培养。\nD) 偷换概念\n首段 ambitious 是描述「做这件事很有抱负」的修饰，而 D 项把它改写成 Encourage\nstudents to be ambitious——把对学校行为的形容词搬给学生，主语和指向都被\n偷换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author think about school field trips?", "options": ["A) They enable students from different backgrounds to mix with each other.", "B) They widen the gap between privileged and disadvantaged students.", "C) They give the disadvantaged students a chance to see the world.", "D) They only benefit students with rich relatives and neighbours."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author think about school field trips? 问的是作者明确表达的看\n法，原文应有直接评价句可定位。\n【拆句】结构简单：主句 What does the author think，介词 about 引出对象 school field trips；\n理解为「作者对校外考察怎么看」即可，无陷阱。\n【定位】Meanwhile, pupils arrive at school hungry because their families can't afford breakfast. The\nChild Poverty Action Group says nine out of 30 in every classroom fall below the poverty\nline. The discrepancy is startlingly apparent.\n与此同时，有些学生因家里供不起早餐而饿着肚子来上学。儿童贫困行动小组称每 30 名学\n生中有 9 名生活在贫困线以下。这种落差触目惊心。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文强调 trips 在富裕与贫困学生间制造割裂，A 项却说 enable students from\ndifferent backgrounds to mix with each other，把「加剧分化」反说成「促进\n交融」。\nB) ✓ 正确\nThe discrepancy is startlingly apparent ↔\nwiden the gap between privileged and disadvantaged students\nbetter-off children can tap up richer aunts and neighbours ↔\nprivileged and disadvantaged students\n预判中的「放大贫富差距」完全对应 widen the gap between privileged and\ndisadvantaged students；其余三项都漏掉了作者明确表达的「扩大差距」这一核心负\n面态度。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文明确指出贫困家庭支付不起 Parents are being asked for thousands of pounds，\ntrips 反而把贫困学生挡在门外；C 项却说 give the disadvantaged students a chance\nto see the world。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文 better-off children can tap up richer aunts and neighbours 只是举出筹款机制\n为何失灵的一个细节，D 项却把它推广为 trips 整体 only benefit students with rich\nrelatives and neighbours。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author suggest can help build community spirit?", "options": ["A) Events aiming to improve community services.", "B) Activities that help to fuel students' ingenuity.", "C) Events that require mutual understanding.", "D) Activities involving all students on campus."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author suggest can help build community spirit? 询问的是作者提\n出的具体手段，原文应有「某做法→促成 community spirit」的直接陈述。\n【拆句】What 既作主句宾语又作 build 的逻辑宾语；宾语从句 What does the author suggest\ncan help build community spirit? 读作「作者建议什么能帮助构建社区精神」即可，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Methods such as whole-school fundraising, with the proceeds pooled, can help to extend\nopportunities and fuel community spirit.\n诸如全校共同筹款、所得款项集中调配等做法，有助于扩大机会、并激发社区凝聚力。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文做法是 whole-school fundraising——校内为校内学生筹款，A 项把它换成\nEvents aiming to improve community services——为社区服务，对象/目的全错。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 ingenuity 是形容学校自身的应对智慧 remarkable determination and\ningenuity，B 项把它移到学生身上 fuel students' ingenuity，主语偷换且与凝聚力议\n题无关。\nC) 无中生有\n原文未提及「相互理解」，作者只讲全校共担费用的筹款机制；require mutual\nunderstanding 是凭常识脑补出的成分。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwhole-school fundraising ↔ Activities involving all students on campus\nwith the proceeds pooled ↔ Activities involving all students on campus\n预判强调的「全校共同参与」在四个选项中仅 Activities involving all students on\ncampus 用 all students 抓住了 whole-school 的核心范围语义。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do we learn about low-income parents regarding school field trips?", "options": ["A) They want their children to participate even though they don't see much benefit.", "B) They don't want their kids to participate but find it hard to keep them from going.", "C) They don't want their kids to miss any chance to broaden their horizons despite the cost.", "D) They want their children to experience adventures but they don't want them to run risks."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about low-income parents regarding school field trips? 问的\n是关于低收入家长的可被原文直接陈述的客观情况。\n【拆句】主干为 What do we learn about low-income parents，介词短语 regarding school\nfield trips 限定话题范围；读作「关于低收入家长在校外考察这事上我们知道什么」即可，\n无陷阱。\n【定位】Even parents who can see that a trip is little more than a party or celebration may well feel\nguilt that their child is left behind.\n即便那些清楚某次行程不过是聚会或庆祝活动的家长，也很可能因为孩子被落下而感到\n愧疚。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na trip is little more than a party or celebration ↔ they don't see much benefit\nmay well feel guilt that their child is left behind ↔ want their children to participate\nwant their children to participate even though they don't see much benefit 中\neven though 直接呼应原文 Even；两块语义「想让孩子参加 + 看不到太多益处」刚好\n同时落在定位句两端。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文家长是「希望孩子参加但有愧」，B 项却说 don't want their kids to participate\nbut find it hard to keep them from going，把家长意愿反过来。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文中真心相信 trip 能开拓眼界的是作者自己 Probing the rock pools of a local\nbeach…can fire children's passions, boost their skills and open their eyes to life's\npossibilities，第四段的家长则是 can see that a trip is little more than a party or\ncelebration；C 项却把「渴望孩子开拓眼界」的态度强加给低收入家长。\nD) 无中生有\n原文没有任何关于 they don't want them to run risks 的内容，「风险/安全」是从\nadventure 一词常识联想出来的，作者完全未提。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the author's expectation of schools?", "options": ["A) Bringing a community together with ingenuity.", "B) Resolving the existing discrepancies in society.", "C) Avoiding creating new gaps among students.", "D) Giving poor students preferential treatment."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the author's expectation of schools? 问的是作者表态性的具体期待，可由\n文末作者直接陈述句定位。\n【拆句】主干 What is the author's expectation of schools，of schools 是对象状语；读作「作者\n对学校的期待是什么」即可，无陷阱。\n【定位】Schools cannot be expected to bring together communities single-handed. But the\nleast we should expect is that they do not foster divisions and exclude those who are\nalready disadvantaged.\n我们不能指望学校独力把不同社群凝聚起来。但我们至少该期待的是：学校不要去制造分\n裂、不要去把那些本已处于弱势的学生排除在外。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰说 Schools cannot be expected to bring together communities single-\nhanded，A 项却把它说成期待 Bringing a community together with ingenuity。\nB) 过度推断\n作者只要求学校不要制造新分裂，并未要求他们 Resolving the existing discrepancies\nin society——解决既有社会差距已超出作者明确表态。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthey do not foster divisions ↔ Avoiding creating new gaps among students\nexclude those who are already disadvantaged ↔ new gaps among students\n预判强调的「避免制造新差距、排斥弱势」在选项中仅 Avoiding creating new gaps\namong students 以 Avoiding 显式保留了 do not 这一否定底线。\nD) 无中生有\n原文未要求学校对贫困生 Giving poor students preferential treatment，只要求不要\n排斥；preferential 这一额外优待是作者未提的成分。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What will happen by 2100, according to a new study?", "options": ["A) King penguins in the Antarctic will be on the verge of dying out.", "B) Sea water will rise to a much higher level around the Antarctic.", "C) The melting ice cover will destroy the great Antarctic wilderness.", "D) The pristine waters around the Antarctic will disappear forever."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What will happen by 2100 是对特定时间点将发生事件的客观询问，没有 infer、\nsuggest、imply 等推断信号词。\n【拆句】What will happen by 2100, according to a new study? 主干是 What will happen，by\n2100 是时间状语，according to a new study 是来源状语，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Rising temperatures and overfishing in the pristine(未受污染的) waters around the Antarctic\ncould see king penguin populations pushed to the brink of extinction by the end of the\ncentury, according to a new study.\n据一项新研究，南极未受污染水域的气温上升与过度捕捞，可能使帝企鹅种群在本世纪末濒\n临灭绝。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nking penguin populations pushed to the brink of extinction ↔\nKing penguins in the Antarctic will be on the verge of dying out.\n预判要的「濒临灭绝」与 on the verge of dying out 形成短语级的等值同义替换，且\n时间状语 by 2100 与原文一致。\nB) 无中生有\nSea water will rise to a much higher level around the Antarctic. 讲的是海平面上\n升，原文只说 Rising temperatures（气温上升），没说海平面上升。\nC) 偷换概念\nThe melting ice cover will destroy the great Antarctic wilderness. 说南极荒野将被\n冰盖融化摧毁。原文虽提到 world's last great wilderness，但说的是企鹅可能灭绝，而\n非荒野被毁；melting ice cover 也未在文中作为 2100 年的预测出现。\nD) 与原文相反\nThe pristine waters around the Antarctic will disappear forever. 说南极纯净水域将\n永远消失。原文 pristine\\(未受污染的\\) waters around the Antarctic 只是被借来限定\n位置，根本没说这片水域会消失，反而是研究警告水域里的生物会消失。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn from the findings of a separate study?", "options": ["A) Shrinking krill population and rising temperatures could force Antarctic whales to migrate.", "B) Human activities have accelerated climate change in the Antarctic region in recent years.", "C) Industrial fishing and climate change could be fatal to certain Antarctic species.", "D) Krill fishing in the Antarctic has worsened the pollution of the pristine waters."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the findings of a separate study? 问的是另一项研究的具体结\n论，客观转述即可。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the findings of a separate study? 主干 What do we learn，\nfrom the findings of a separate study 整体作介词短语状语，无复杂从句、无否定，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Earlier this month a separate study found that a combination of climate change and industrial\nfishing is threatening the krill(磷虾) population in Antarctic waters, with a potentially\ndisastrous impact on whales, seals and penguins.\n本月早些时候另一项独立研究发现，气候变化和工业捕捞的共同作用正威胁着南极水域的磷\n虾种群，可能对鲸、海豹和企鹅造成灾难性影响。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nShrinking krill population and rising temperatures could force Antarctic whales\nto migrate. 把鲸迁徙写成结论，但原文只说对鲸有 disastrous impact，并未说鲸会\n迁徙。\nB) 答非所问\nHuman activities have accelerated climate change in the Antarctic region in\nrecent years. 说人类活动加速了气候变化，是另一个层面的话题，原 separate study\n的结论不是这个。\nC) ✓ 正确\na combination of climate change and industrial fishing ↔\nIndustrial fishing and climate change\nwith a potentially disastrous impact on whales, seals and penguins ↔\ncould be fatal to certain Antarctic species\n「合力」和「灾难性影响」两个核心节点均被 C 项以同义替换形式覆盖，且范围词\ncertain 与原文 whales, seals and penguins 的有限枚举一致。\nD) 无中生有\nKrill fishing in the Antarctic has worsened the pollution of the pristine waters. 说\n磷虾捕捞恶化了水污染，原文从未谈水域被污染，只是用 pristine 形容尚未污染。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the passage say about king penguins?", "options": ["A) They will turn out to be the second-largest species of birds to become extinct.", "B) Many of them will have to migrate to isolated islands in the Southern Ocean.", "C) They feed primarily on only a few kinds of krill in the Antarctic Polar Front.", "D) The majority of them may have to find new breeding grounds in the future."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about king penguins? 问的是「文章直接说了什么」，不是\ninfer 也不是 main idea，属于针对核心对象的客观信息题。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about king penguins? 主干 What does the passage say，\nabout king penguins 介宾短语作 say 的宾语补足说明，无从句无否定，无陷阱。\n【定位】Unless current greenhouse gas emissions drop, 70 percent of king penguins—1.1 million\nbreeding pairs—will be forced to relocate their breeding grounds, or face extinction by 2100.\n除非当前温室气体排放下降，否则 70\\% 的帝企鹅——110 万对繁殖伴侣——将被迫迁移\n繁殖地，或在 2100 年面临灭绝。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThey will turn out to be the second-largest species of birds to become extinct. 把\n原文 King penguins are the second-largest type of penguin（第二大企鹅种类）偷\n换成「第二大将灭绝的鸟类」。\nB) 与原文相反\nMany of them will have to migrate to isolated islands in the Southern Ocean. 说\n企鹅将迁往孤立岛屿，可原文 only breed on specific isolated islands in the\nSouthern Ocean 是说它们本来就只在这些岛上繁殖，不是迁过去。\nC) 以偏概全\nThey feed primarily on only a few kinds of krill in the Antarctic Polar Front. 说企鹅\n主要只吃几种磷虾，原文 king penguins, which feed on fish and krill in this body of\nwater 明明是鱼和磷虾两类，且没说限定几种。\nD) ✓ 正确\n70 percent of king penguins ↔ The majority of them\nwill be forced to relocate their breeding grounds ↔\nmay have to find new breeding grounds in the future\n数量节点（70\\% ↔ majority）和动作节点（被迫迁繁殖地 ↔ 找新繁殖地）双双等值，\n锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What happens when sea levels rise in the Antarctic?", "options": ["A) Many baby king penguins can't have food in time.", "B) Many king penguins could no longer live on krill.", "C) Whales will invade king penguins' breeding grounds.", "D) Whales will have to travel long distances to find food."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What happens when sea levels rise in the Antarctic? 问的是某条件发生时的直接客观后\n果，不涉及推断或态度。\n【拆句】What happens when sea levels rise in the Antarctic? 主干 What happens，when sea\nlevels rise in the Antarctic 是 when 引导的时间/条件状语从句，结构清晰。注意 sea\nlevels rise 是题干的转述，原文真正描述的是「ocean warms（海洋升温）」，需要按因果\n链而非字面去匹配。\n【定位】As the ocean warms, a body of water called the Antarctic Polar Front—an upward movement\nof nutrient-rich sea that supports a huge abundance of marine life—is being pushed further\nsouth. This means that king penguins, which feed on fish and krill in this body of water, have\nto travel further to their feeding grounds, leaving their hungry chicks for longer.\n随着海洋升温，名为南极极锋的水体——一股富含营养的海水上涌、支撑着大量海洋生物\n——正在被推向更南方。这意味着以这片水域中的鱼和磷虾为食的帝企鹅必须前往更远处觅\n食，使饥饿的幼鸟被独留更长时间。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nleaving their hungry chicks for longer ↔ Many baby king penguins can't have food in time.\n预判要的「幼鸟挨饿」与 can't have food in time 完全等值；其余选项都偏离这条因果\n链的末端。\nB) 过度推断\nMany king penguins could no longer live on krill. 说企鹅再也不能靠磷虾为生，但原\n文只说他们要跑更远去吃，不是再也吃不到。\nC) 无中生有\nWhales will invade king penguins' breeding grounds. 说鲸入侵企鹅繁殖地，全文未\n提此事。\nD) 张冠李戴\nWhales will have to travel long distances to find food. 描述了鲸要长途觅食，但原\n文长途觅食的是 king penguins 而不是鲸。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2019-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What do we learn about the Southern Ocean?", "options": ["A) The king penguins there are reluctant to leave for new breeding grounds.", "B) Its conservation is key to the sustainable propagation of Antarctic species.", "C) It is most likely to become the ultimate retreat for species like the king penguin.", "D) Only a few of its islands can serve as huge breeding grounds for king penguins."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about the Southern Ocean? 问的是关于南大洋的客观信息，无推断/\n态度信号词。\n【拆句】What do we learn about the Southern Ocean? 主干 What do we learn，about the\nSouthern Ocean 介宾短语限定范围，无从句无否定，无陷阱。\n【定位】Only a handful of islands in the Southern Ocean are suitable for sustaining large\nbreeding colonies.\n南大洋只有少数岛屿适合维持大规模的繁殖群落。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThe king penguins there are reluctant to leave for new breeding grounds. 说企鹅\n不愿离开，原文从未描述企鹅的意愿，只说迁徙是「被迫」。\nB) 过度推断\nIts conservation is key to the sustainable propagation of Antarctic species. 说南大\n洋的保护对南极物种可持续繁衍至关重要。原文只说企鹅是 bio-indicators 而未谈「南\n大洋保护是关键」。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt is most likely to become the ultimate retreat for species like the king penguin.\n说南大洋很可能成为终极避难所，原文反而说「合适的新栖息地稀缺」，方向与之\n相悖。\nD) ✓ 正确\nOnly a handful of islands in the Southern Ocean ↔ Only a few of its islands\nare suitable for sustaining large breeding colonies ↔\ncan serve as huge breeding grounds for king penguins\n量的限定（only a handful ↔ only a few）与功能描述（suitable for sustaining ↔\ncan serve as）均有等值落地，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2019-12-1"});
